# OXPlayer Override Strategy

`oxplayer-client` is a fork of **Fladder** (Jellyfin Flutter client).
The goal is to keep upstream files nearly unchanged so the repo can be rebased on Fladder updates with minimal conflict.

## The golden rule

> **Never delete or replace upstream code. Add OX behavior beside it, gated behind `OxplayerConfig.isEnabled`.**

## Where new code lives

All OX-specific business logic, screens, and utilities go under **`lib/oxplayer/`**.
Do **not** scatter OX logic across `lib/screens/`, `lib/providers/`, `lib/widgets/`, etc.

```
lib/
  oxplayer/               ← OX-only code
    providers/            ← OX + Riverpod API state (new; see "Riverpod" below)
    oxplayer_config.dart  ← OxplayerConfig.isEnabled flag
    oxplayer_env.dart     ← all env/config values
    oxplayer_bootstrap.dart ← startup hooks
    oxplayer_navigation.dart ← route overrides
    ...
  screens/                ← upstream Fladder (touch as little as possible)
  providers/              ← upstream Fladder (Jellyfin + shared @riverpod)
```

## OX + Riverpod for HTTP / API (retouch and new OX work)

For **new or refactored** OX code that calls the OX server (`/me/chats*`, future OX-only REST), use **[Riverpod](https://riverpod.dev/)** (`@riverpod` from `package:riverpod_annotation`) so async state, **loading / error / data**, **invalidation**, and tests stay out of widget `build` methods. Upstream **Fladder** can stay on hand-written `Provider`s in `lib/providers/`; OX can evolve toward the same code-gen style already used in this repo (e.g. `lib/providers/seerr_*.dart`).

### Substructure (under `lib/oxplayer/`)

| Location | Role |
|----------|------|
| `oxplayer/providers/ox_my_telegram_api.dart` | Scaffolding: re-exports pattern for the signed-in `OxplayerUserChatsClient?`. Add new `@riverpod` functions/classes **next to** this file (e.g. `my_telegram_indexed_media.dart`) as features grow. |
| `oxplayer/providers/*.g.dart` | **Generated** — do not hand-edit; run `dart run build_runner build` after changing `@riverpod` sources. |
| OX **screens** in `oxplayer/**/` | `ref.watch` / `ref.listen` providers; **no** direct `http.get` / raw client calls in deep widgets — take `Ref` / `WidgetRef` at the top of the OX feature and delegate to a provider. |
| `oxplayerUserChatsClientProvider` | Remains the low-level `Provider` for the authed `OxplayerUserChatsClient`; OX Riverpod notifiers can `ref.watch` that or the scaffold `myTelegramOxChatsClientProvider` in `ox_my_telegram_api.g.dart`. |

### Patterns to follow

- Prefer **`@riverpod` + `part '*.g.dart'`** for anything that fetches, paginates, or must **invalidate** after an action (e.g. ingest) — use `ref.invalidate(...)` on a **family** provider or bump a `AsyncNotifier` instead of ad-hoc `StateProvider` counters when the refactor is done.
- The **“API on the server”** rule *below* still applies: wrong or missing DTOs are fixed in `oxplayer/apps/api/`, not papered over in a Riverpod layer.

## Patching upstream files

When an upstream file needs OX behavior, use the **thin hook** pattern — keep the upstream call intact and branch around it:

```dart
// ✅ GOOD — upstream code kept, OX behavior added beside it
void onSignOut() {
  if (OxplayerConfig.isEnabled) {
    context.router.replaceAll(oxplayerSignOutRouteList());
    return;
  }
  context.router.replaceAll([LoginRoute()]);   // original upstream line
}

// ❌ BAD — upstream code replaced or deleted
void onSignOut() {
  context.router.replaceAll([OxplayerLoginRoute()]);
}
```

The only upstream files that should contain OX hooks are thin entry points:
`main.dart`, `app_bootstrap.dart`, and similar root files — and only via `OxplayerBootstrap`.

## OxplayerBootstrap lifecycle

```dart
// main.dart / bootstrapper
await OxplayerBootstrap.beforeAppBootstrap(args);   // env, experiments
await bootstrapApplication(...);
await OxplayerBootstrap.afterAppBootstrap(result);  // URL sync, session
```

Add new startup work inside `OxplayerBootstrap`, not in `main.dart` directly.

## Config / env values

Always read config from `OxplayerEnv` (never hardcode URLs or keys):

```dart
final apiUrl = OxplayerEnv.apiBaseUrl;        // OXPLAYER_API_BASE_URL
final botUser = OxplayerEnv.botUsername;      // BOT_USERNAME / OXPLAYER_BOT_USERNAME
final appId = OxplayerEnv.telegramApiId;      // TELEGRAM_API_ID
```

`OxplayerConfig.isEnabled` is `true` by default; disable with `--dart-define=OXPLAYER=false`.

## API data contract — fix on the server, not the client

> **The server owns the schema. The client must not patch, transform, or work around malformed API responses.**

If a field is missing, has the wrong type, or points to the wrong URL:
- **Fix it in `oxplayer/apps/api/`** — correct the DTO, the route handler, or the sync logic.
- **Do not** add client-side workarounds in `lib/models/`, `lib/providers/`, or `lib/screens/` to compensate for bad server output.

Concrete examples of what this means:

| Situation | ✅ Right place to fix | ❌ Wrong place |
|---|---|---|
| Image URL goes to the server instead of TMDB CDN | Server: return `302 → image.tmdb.org` from the image route | Client: special-case the URL in `ImageData.imageProvider` |
| Logo request causes timeout | Server: handle `/Images/Logo` and redirect to TMDB primary | Client: skip logo in `ImagesData.fromBaseItem` |
| Backdrop not loading | Server: set `BackdropImageTags` + redirect `/Images/Backdrop/:i` to TMDB | Client: fall back to `ExternalUrls` in the model |
| Item DTO missing a field the UI needs | Server: add the field to `toBaseItemDto` | Client: derive it at render time |

The only client-side infrastructure allowed in `lib/oxplayer/` is transport-layer concerns that cannot live on the server — e.g. `OxplayerCacheManager` (custom HTTP client for ngrok headers) or auth token injection.

## Avoid

- Adding `OxplayerConfig.isEnabled` checks inside deep widget trees or providers — push that up to the hook site.
- Creating files under `lib/oxplayer/` for logic that belongs in upstream Fladder unchanged.
- Modifying generated code (`jellyfin_open_api.swagger*.dart`) — those are regenerated from the spec.
- Compensating for missing or wrong API data anywhere in the Flutter codebase — fix the API instead.
